 "notes": [
  "Historical window 1995-2014 (experiment 'historical'); mid-century window 2041-2060 under ssp245 (middle-of-the-road emissions) and ssp585 (high emissions).",
  "Each change is one model's mid-century mean minus the same model's historical mean; the summary gives the median across models, the middle half (p25-p75) and the full range (min-max).",
  "April-July precipitation: daily precipitation (kg m-2 s-1 x 86400 = mm/day) averaged over the grid cells with area weights, summed over April-July; percent change is relative to the model's own historical mean. Inches = mm / 25.4.",
  "Hot days: June-July days with daily maximum temperature at or above 90 F (305.37 K), counted in each grid cell separately and then area-weighted, i.e. the count at a typical spot in the county (not the count of the county-averaged series, which would smooth extremes).",
  "Mean temperature: annual mean of (daily max + daily min) / 2, area-weighted; changes in F = changes in C x 9/5.",
  "Grid cells: every 0.25 degree cell overlapping the county outline, weighted by the share of the county it covers (cos-latitude area).",
  "File versions: the newest version NCCS lists for each file (v1.1 or v2.0 where available); Planetary Computer hosts the original v1.0 files.",
  "Bias correction makes each model's historical climate close to the observation-based reference, so the baseline describes the reference climate; trust the changes, not small baseline differences.",
  "The baseline runs warmer than local Daymet: Daymet 1995-2014 gives a mean of about 48 F, 9 June-July hot days and 5.9 in April-July rain near Pullman, and 51 F, 17-18 hot days and 4.0 in near LaCrosse (checked 2026-11-15). Do not show the baseline next to Daymet as if they were the same measure; show the changes.",
